---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PromptPage from "../../../components/PromptPage.astro";

const masterPrompt = `Act as an expert direct-response copywriter, paid-media strategist and conversion-focused marketing writer.

TASK:
Create clear, persuasive and platform-appropriate ad copy for the supplied product, service, offer or campaign.

The copy should:
- match the target audience
- support the stated campaign objective
- reflect the audience's awareness stage
- communicate real customer value
- fit the selected advertising platform
- use strong but truthful hooks
- avoid unsupported claims
- include practical A/B test variations
- remain consistent with the brand voice

BUSINESS / BRAND:
[Name and short description.]

PRODUCT / SERVICE:
[What are you advertising?]

TARGET AUDIENCE:
[Who should see the ad?]

MARKET / LOCATION:
[Country / city / region / online/global.]

CAMPAIGN OBJECTIVE:
[Awareness / traffic / leads / sales / app installs / bookings / downloads / other.]

AWARENESS STAGE:
[Unaware / problem-aware / solution-aware / product-aware / most aware / unknown.]

CUSTOMER PAIN:
[Main problem.]

CUSTOMER DESIRE:
[Desired outcome.]

VALUE PROPOSITION:
[Why should the customer care?]

KEY BENEFITS:
[List supported benefits.]

KEY FEATURES:
[List relevant real features.]

DIFFERENTIATORS:
[Real meaningful differences.]

OFFER:
[Discount / free trial / free tool / consultation / bundle / none.]

PROOF:
[Reviews / data / demonstration / case study / credentials / none.]

PLATFORM:
[Facebook / Instagram / LinkedIn / Google Search / display / TikTok / YouTube / other.]

AD FORMAT:
[Feed ad / Reel ad / Story ad / search ad / image ad / carousel / video ad / other.]

DESTINATION:
[Landing page / product page / lead form / app / store / message / other.]

PRIMARY CTA:
[Buy / learn more / book / sign up / download / calculate / contact / other.]

BRAND TONE:
[Professional / friendly / premium / practical / energetic / direct / educational / other.]

LENGTH:
[Short / medium / long / platform appropriate.]

RESTRICTIONS:
[Legal, compliance, platform, brand or wording restrictions.]

SPECIAL REQUIREMENTS:
[Any other instructions.]

AD COPY REQUIREMENTS:

1. START WITH THE CAMPAIGN OBJECTIVE
The copy must support the intended action.

2. UNDERSTAND THE AUDIENCE BEFORE WRITING

3. IDENTIFY THE PRIMARY MOTIVATION
Focus on the strongest relevant pain, desire or use case.

4. DO NOT TRY TO SELL EVERY BENEFIT AT ONCE

5. USE ONE PRIMARY MESSAGE PER AD VARIANT

6. MATCH THE AWARENESS STAGE

7. UNAWARE AUDIENCE
Lead with a relatable situation, observation or problem signal.

8. PROBLEM-AWARE AUDIENCE
Lead with the pain and consequence.

9. SOLUTION-AWARE AUDIENCE
Lead with the approach or category of solution.

10. PRODUCT-AWARE AUDIENCE
Lead with differentiation, proof, feature or offer.

11. MOST-AWARE AUDIENCE
Lead directly with the offer, CTA or reason to act.

12. IF AWARENESS STAGE IS UNKNOWN
Create variants for more than one plausible stage.

13. CREATE MULTIPLE HOOK ANGLES

14. POSSIBLE ANGLES:
Problem
Outcome
Question
Contrarian insight
Demonstration
Use case
Mistake
Comparison
Convenience
Time saving
Cost control
Risk reduction
Educational
Offer

15. KEEP HOOKS TRUTHFUL

16. DO NOT USE FALSE CURIOSITY

17. DO NOT USE MISLEADING CLICKBAIT

18. DO NOT USE FALSE URGENCY

19. DO NOT USE FALSE SCARCITY

20. DO NOT INVENT LIMITED-TIME OFFERS

21. DO NOT INVENT DISCOUNTS

22. DO NOT INVENT STOCK LIMITATIONS

23. DO NOT INVENT CUSTOMER RESULTS

24. DO NOT INVENT TESTIMONIALS

25. DO NOT INVENT RATINGS

26. DO NOT INVENT AWARDS

27. DO NOT INVENT CERTIFICATIONS

28. DO NOT INVENT MARKET-LEADER CLAIMS

29. DO NOT INVENT QUANTIFIED PERFORMANCE CLAIMS

30. USE CLAIMS ONLY WHEN SUPPORTED

31. LABEL UNVERIFIED CLAIMS

32. SOFTEN CLAIMS WHEN NECESSARY

33. EXAMPLE:
Avoid:
"Never run out of stock again."

Prefer:
"Use a structured reorder-point calculation to support better replenishment decisions."

34. DISTINGUISH FEATURE FROM BENEFIT

35. FEATURE:
What the product has or does.

36. BENEFIT:
Why the feature matters to the customer.

37. OUTCOME:
What improved situation the customer wants.

38. CONNECT FEATURE → BENEFIT → OUTCOME

39. USE CUSTOMER LANGUAGE
Prefer natural wording over internal business terminology.

40. AVOID CORPORATE JARGON

41. AVOID GENERIC CLAIMS SUCH AS:
Game-changing
Revolutionary
World-class
Ultimate
Best-in-class
Next-level

unless clearly supported and appropriate.

42. CREATE CLEAR HEADLINES

43. HEADLINES SHOULD PRIORITIZE:
Clarity
Relevance
Specificity
Value

44. DO NOT MAKE HEADLINES CLEVER AT THE EXPENSE OF UNDERSTANDING

45. CREATE PRIMARY TEXT THAT ADDS INFORMATION
Do not simply repeat the headline.

46. CREATE A CLEAR CTA

47. CTA MUST MATCH CUSTOMER READINESS

48. LOW-COMMITMENT CTA OPTIONS:
Learn More
See How It Works
Explore
Try It
View Details

49. LEAD CTA OPTIONS:
Request Quote
Book Consultation
Get Estimate
Sign Up
Register

50. SALES CTA OPTIONS:
Buy Now
Order Now
Start Trial
Shop Now

51. TOOL / RESOURCE CTA OPTIONS:
Calculate Now
Download
Use the Tool
Get the Template

52. DO NOT USE "BUY NOW" FOR A TOP-OF-FUNNEL EDUCATIONAL AD UNLESS APPROPRIATE

53. FACEBOOK / INSTAGRAM MODE
Provide:
Primary text
Headline
Description if useful
CTA
Hook
Visual direction

54. FACEBOOK COPY SHOULD BE MOBILE-FIRST
The strongest idea should appear early.

55. INSTAGRAM COPY SHOULD WORK WITH VISUAL-FIRST CREATIVE

56. LINKEDIN MODE
Focus on:
Professional relevance
Business problem
Operational impact
Credible value
Clear CTA

57. AVOID OVERLY CASUAL LINKEDIN LANGUAGE UNLESS THE BRAND CALLS FOR IT

58. GOOGLE SEARCH MODE
Provide:
Headline options
Description options
Keyword-intent alignment
CTA

59. SEARCH AD COPY SHOULD MATCH SEARCH INTENT

60. DO NOT WRITE BROAD BRAND COPY FOR HIGH-INTENT SEARCH TERMS

61. DISPLAY MODE
Use:
Short headline
Supporting line
Clear value
Simple CTA

62. TIKTOK / SHORT-FORM AD MODE
Use:
Immediate hook
Natural spoken language
Demonstration or use case
Fast value
Simple CTA

63. YOUTUBE MODE
Create:
Opening hook
Problem / context
Value
Proof or demonstration
CTA

64. VIDEO ADS SHOULD ALIGN COPY WITH WHAT IS VISIBLE

65. DO NOT SAY SOMETHING THE CREATIVE DOES NOT SUPPORT

66. CREATE VARIANTS BY ANGLE

67. VARIANT A
Problem-led.

68. VARIANT B
Outcome-led.

69. VARIANT C
Feature / demonstration-led.

70. VARIANT D
Differentiation-led.

71. VARIANT E
Offer-led, only when a real offer exists.

72. DO NOT CREATE FIVE VERSIONS THAT ONLY CHANGE A FEW WORDS

73. EACH VARIANT SHOULD TEST A MEANINGFUL STRATEGIC DIFFERENCE

74. CREATE A/B TESTS

75. CHANGE ONE PRIMARY VARIABLE AT A TIME

76. POSSIBLE TEST VARIABLES:
Hook
Headline
Benefit
CTA
Visual concept
Offer framing

77. DO NOT CHANGE AUDIENCE, OFFER, HOOK AND CTA ALL AT ONCE IN A CONTROLLED A/B TEST

78. IDENTIFY THE TEST HYPOTHESIS

79. EXAMPLE:
"Problem-led copy may outperform feature-led copy for problem-aware users."

80. MATCH COPY LENGTH TO PLATFORM

81. SHORT COPY
Use when:
Visual explains most of the message
Audience is familiar
Offer is simple

82. LONGER COPY
Use when:
Product needs explanation
Risk is higher
Audience needs education

83. DO NOT MAKE COPY LONG JUST TO SOUND PERSUASIVE

84. KEEP SENTENCES READABLE

85. USE PARAGRAPH BREAKS FOR MOBILE

86. AVOID EXCESSIVE EMOJIS

87. USE EMOJIS ONLY WHEN THEY FIT THE BRAND AND PLATFORM

88. DO NOT OVERUSE CAPITAL LETTERS

89. DO NOT OVERUSE EXCLAMATION MARKS

90. CREATE VISUAL-COPY ALIGNMENT
For each ad, suggest what should appear visually.

91. VISUAL SHOULD SUPPORT THE CLAIM

92. DEMONSTRATION ADS
Show the actual product or process where possible.

93. PROBLEM ADS
Show a recognizable situation without exaggerating distress.

94. OUTCOME ADS
Show a plausible desired state without implying guaranteed results.

95. PRODUCT ADS
Preserve product identity, packaging and real features.

96. BEFORE / AFTER ADS
Use only when truthful and compliant.

97. CREATE MESSAGE HIERARCHY
Hook
Primary value
Supporting detail
Proof
CTA

98. DO NOT INTRODUCE PROOF WHEN NONE EXISTS

99. IF PROOF IS AVAILABLE
Use it proportionally.

100. TESTIMONIALS
Only use exact approved testimonials supplied by the user.

101. DO NOT FABRICATE QUOTED CUSTOMER LANGUAGE

102. CREATE OBJECTION-AWARE COPY WHEN USEFUL

103. COMMON OBJECTIONS:
Price
Complexity
Trust
Time
Switching effort
Uncertainty

104. ADDRESS ONLY RELEVANT OBJECTIONS

105. DO NOT CREATE DEFENSIVE COPY THAT INTRODUCES NEW CONCERNS

106. CREATE RETARGETING COPY WHEN REQUESTED

107. RETARGETING COPY MAY EMPHASIZE:
Reminder
Unfinished action
Product benefit
Proof
Offer
FAQ

108. DO NOT CLAIM TO KNOW WHY AN INDIVIDUAL USER DID NOT CONVERT

109. CREATE LOCAL AD COPY WHEN LOCATION MATTERS

110. LOCAL COPY MAY INCLUDE:
Service area
Delivery area
Store location
Local need

111. VERIFY ALL LOCATION CLAIMS

112. CREATE B2B COPY WHEN APPROPRIATE

113. B2B COPY SHOULD CONNECT TO:
Workflow
Cost
Time
Risk
Control
Revenue
Productivity
Decision quality

114. DO NOT CLAIM ROI WITHOUT EVIDENCE

115. CREATE B2C COPY WHEN APPROPRIATE

116. B2C COPY MAY EMPHASIZE:
Convenience
Experience
Time
Confidence
Value
Practical outcome

117. HANDLE FREE PRODUCTS CAREFULLY
"Free" can be a valid benefit when genuinely free.

118. DO NOT ADD:
No credit card
No signup
Unlimited
Forever free

unless supplied and true.

119. HANDLE PRICE CAREFULLY
Use exact supplied pricing only.

120. DO NOT INFER DISCOUNTS FROM PRICE DIFFERENCES

121. CREATE COMPLIANCE FLAGS
Identify wording that could be risky.

122. FLAG:
Health claims
Financial claims
Guaranteed results
Employment outcomes
Income claims
Before-and-after claims
Personal-attribute targeting
Sensitive topics
Unsupported comparisons

123. DO NOT MAKE POLICY APPROVAL GUARANTEES
Platform rules can change and final approval belongs to the platform.

124. CREATE CHARACTER-LENGTH AWARENESS
For platforms with tight formats, keep versions concise.

125. DO NOT CLAIM EXACT PLATFORM LIMITS UNLESS PROVIDED OR VERIFIED

126. PROVIDE SHORTER BACKUP VERSIONS

127. CREATE CREATIVE BRIEFS
For each major ad concept provide:
Visual
Opening frame
On-screen text
Primary action
CTA frame

128. KEEP ON-SCREEN TEXT SHORT

129. DO NOT RELY ON AI-GENERATED TEXT INSIDE IMAGES WHEN TEXT ACCURACY IS CRITICAL
Recommend manual text overlay where appropriate.

130. CREATE LANDING-PAGE MESSAGE ALIGNMENT
Ad promise should match the destination.

131. DO NOT USE AN AD HOOK THAT THE LANDING PAGE DOES NOT DELIVER ON

132. IDENTIFY MESSAGE GAPS
If the supplied value proposition or offer is weak, say so.

133. DO NOT COMPENSATE FOR A WEAK OFFER WITH EXAGGERATED COPY

134. CREATE A CLAIM CHECK

135. FOR EACH IMPORTANT CLAIM PROVIDE:
Claim
Evidence supplied
Risk level
Recommended wording

136. CREATE A FINAL COPY REVIEW

137. CHECK FOR:
Clarity
Relevance
Credibility
Specificity
Platform fit
Brand fit
CTA clarity
Claim support

138. REMOVE REDUNDANCY

139. REMOVE GENERIC FILLER

140. FINAL QUALITY CHECK
Before finalizing verify:

- objective is clear
- audience is clear
- awareness stage is considered
- primary motivation is clear
- value proposition is represented accurately
- one main message is used per variant
- hooks are truthful
- headlines are clear
- benefits connect to real features
- no features were invented
- no offers were invented
- no testimonials were invented
- no results were invented
- no false urgency was added
- no false scarcity was added
- CTA matches intent
- copy fits the selected platform
- visual direction supports the copy
- A/B variants test meaningful differences
- unsupported claims are flagged
- landing-page alignment is considered
- language is natural
- copy is mobile-readable where relevant

OUTPUT FORMAT:

1. AD STRATEGY SUMMARY
Provide:
Audience
Objective
Awareness stage
Primary pain
Desired outcome
Value proposition
Platform
CTA

2. CORE MESSAGE
State the single strongest message to communicate.

3. AD ANGLES
Create 3–5 distinct strategic angles.

For each provide:
Angle name
Customer insight
Main message
Hook
Why it may work

4. PRIMARY AD COPY
Provide:
Hook
Primary text
Headline
Description if relevant
CTA

5. COPY VARIATIONS
Create distinct versions such as:
Problem-led
Outcome-led
Demonstration-led
Differentiation-led
Offer-led if applicable

6. SHORT COPY VERSIONS
Create concise backup versions.

7. PLATFORM-SPECIFIC VERSION
Adapt the strongest concept to the selected platform and format.

8. VISUAL DIRECTION
Provide:
Opening visual
Main subject
Action
On-screen text
CTA frame

9. A/B TEST PLAN
Provide:
Control
Variant
Variable being tested
Hypothesis
Primary metric

10. OBJECTION VERSION
Create one version that addresses the most important relevant objection.

11. RETARGETING VERSION
Create one retargeting ad if appropriate.

12. CLAIM & COMPLIANCE CHECK
List:
Potentially risky claim
Why it needs attention
Safer wording or required proof

13. LANDING-PAGE ALIGNMENT CHECK
State what the destination page should immediately confirm.

14. FINAL RECOMMENDED AD
Provide the strongest polished version ready for human review.

IMPORTANT:
- Do not invent product facts.
- Do not invent proof.
- Do not invent offers.
- Do not fabricate testimonials.
- Do not manufacture urgency or scarcity.
- Do not promise guaranteed results.
- Match the copy to the audience's awareness stage.
- Keep each ad focused on one primary message.
- Prefer specific customer value over generic persuasion.
- Flag claims that need validation before publication.`;

const steps = [
  {
    title: "Choose one message",
    description:
      "Start with the strongest customer pain, desire or use case and give each ad one clear job instead of trying to communicate every feature at once.",
  },
  {
    title: "Match the awareness stage",
    description:
      "A person discovering the problem needs different copy from someone already comparing products or ready to buy.",
  },
  {
    title: "Adapt to the platform",
    description:
      "Use shorter visual-first copy for social formats, professional relevance for LinkedIn and stronger intent matching for search advertising.",
  },
  {
    title: "Test meaningful differences",
    description:
      "Compare strategic angles such as problem-led versus outcome-led copy rather than changing several unrelated elements at the same time.",
  },
];

const example = {
  input: [
    "Product: Free online Reorder Point Calculator.",
    "Audience: Small-business owners and inventory professionals.",
    "Objective: Website traffic and tool usage.",
    "Platform: LinkedIn.",
    "Pain: Reordering too late or relying on guesswork.",
    "Value: Calculates reorder point from demand, lead time and safety stock.",
    "CTA: Calculate now.",
  ],

  output: [
    "CORE MESSAGE: Use your own inventory inputs to calculate a clearer reorder trigger instead of relying only on guesswork.",
    "PROBLEM-LED HOOK: Still deciding when to reorder stock by instinct?",
    "PRIMARY TEXT: Reordering too late can create avoidable stock pressure, while ordering without a consistent method makes planning harder. Enter your demand, lead time and safety stock into the free calculator to estimate a practical reorder point.",
    "HEADLINE: Know When It's Time to Reorder",
    "CTA: Calculate Now",
    "VISUAL: Show the calculator interface with the three inputs being entered and the reorder-point result appearing clearly.",
    "A/B TEST: Compare the problem-led hook against an outcome-led version while keeping the visual and CTA unchanged.",
    "CLAIM CHECK: Avoid saying the tool prevents stockouts, because actual inventory outcomes also depend on forecast accuracy, supplier reliability and operating conditions.",
  ],
};

const tips = [
  {
    title: "One ad should make one main argument",
    description:
      "Trying to include every feature, benefit and objection usually weakens the message. Build separate variants around different customer motivations instead.",
  },
  {
    title: "A stronger hook does not need exaggeration",
    description:
      "Specific problems, useful questions and visible demonstrations can create attention without unsupported promises or artificial urgency.",
  },
  {
    title: "Test the angle, not random wording",
    description:
      "A useful A/B test compares a meaningful variable such as problem-led versus outcome-led messaging while keeping the rest of the ad reasonably consistent.",
  },
];

---

<BaseLayout
  title="Ad Copy Writer Prompt | KRIYANO"
  description="Create platform-appropriate ad copy with stronger hooks, headlines, CTAs, messaging angles, A/B variants and claim checks."
>
  <PromptPage
    category="MARKETING"
    title="Ad Copy Writer"
    accentTitle="Prompt."
    description="Create focused advertising copy for social, search, display and professional campaigns using real customer value, platform-aware messaging and testable variations."
    meta={[
      "Free prompt",
      "Ad copy",
      "Paid marketing",
    ]}
    bestFor={[
      "Facebook ads",
      "Instagram ads",
      "LinkedIn ads",
      "Search ads",
      "Video ads",
      "A/B testing",
    ]}
    masterPrompt={masterPrompt}
    introTitle="Write ads around one clear reason to act."
    introDescription="Define the audience, awareness stage, objective and value proposition, then build truthful hooks, headlines, primary text, CTAs and testable variations for the selected platform."
    steps={steps}
    exampleTitle="Promote a practical inventory calculator without exaggerated claims."
    example={example}
    tipsTitle="Good ad copy is focused, credible and easy to test."
    tips={tips}
    sidebarTitle="Need stronger positioning first?"
    sidebarDescription="Use KRIYANO's Value Proposition Builder before writing ads when the customer benefit, differentiation or core message is still unclear."
    warningTitle="Persuasive advertising still needs factual support"
    warningText="Verify prices, offers, benefits, testimonials, performance results, comparative statements and other material claims before publishing. Advertising-platform policies and legal requirements may also apply."
  />
</BaseLayout>